TH_0009> and <MATH_0010> such that\n<MATH_0011>, <MATH_0012> and\n<MATH_0013>. We take <MATH_0014>\nwith <MATH_0015> and <MATH_0016> the projection morphisms. This works as <MATH_0017> commutes\nwith fibre products; we omit the verification.\n\n<STRUCT_0003><STRUCT_0004>\nSecond, suppose we are given two objects\n<MATH_0018> and <MATH_0019>\nand morphisms <MATH_0020>.\nWe have to find a morphism <MATH_0021>\nwhich equalizes <MATH_0022> and <MATH_0023>. Let <MATH_0024> be the equalizer of\n<MATH_0025> and <MATH_0026> in the category <MATH_0027>. As <MATH_0028> commutes\nwith equalizers and since <MATH_0029>\nwe obtain a morphism <MATH_0030>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:85defee7d5bfa8c3e69d55d7e8118e75cfaf0ec29a197c67233ece72c9af7c19", "unit_id": "tag:00X4:proof"}
